Compare the two blind cleaned documents using the deterministic span ledger.
Inspect all unique spans, cite only supplied stable IDs, and let the ledger—not
raw length or boilerplate volume—determine the replacement directions.

First distinguish the shared record or complete non-main message from reusable
context. Full faithful translations of either content type are bidirectionally
equivalent, material none, only when every unique span is covered or harmless UI.
Separate a missing same-record field from a conflicting value or another record.
Cite concrete subject/service target, policy scope, state, or page-role changes;
buttons, navigation, language, and whitespace alone do not establish a conflict.
Check every delta even when translation_status is complete_faithful.

<semantic_diff status="{{ semantic_diff.status }}" truncated="{{ semantic_diff.truncated }}">
counts: {{ semantic_diff.span_counts }}
{% for span in semantic_diff.spans %}
{% if span.kind == "SHARED" %}
[{{ span.span_id }} SHARED]
A: {{ span.a_text }}
B: {{ span.b_text }}
{% else %}
[{{ span.span_id }} {{ span.kind }}] {{ span.text }}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
</semantic_diff>
{% if semantic_diff.truncated %}
<truncation_notice>
{% if semantic_diff.truncated_a %}Document A was cut off before its end; content after the visible text above is unknown.
{% endif %}{% if semantic_diff.truncated_b %}Document B was cut off before its end; content after the visible text above is unknown.
{% endif %}A decisive difference could exist only in the cut-off portion. Do not
treat apparent agreement among the visible spans as proof the full documents
match -- use unresolved for any field whose only support is "no difference
was visible in the shown spans," and confidence_tier must not be high.
</truncation_notice>
{% endif %}
